      "name": "strpbrk",
      "signature": "char * strpbrk(char * lpszString, char * lpszAccept)",
      "calling_convention": "__cdecl",
      "comment": "C runtime strpbrk - Locates first occurrence of any character from accept set in string.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Initialize 256-bit bitmap (32 bytes) to all zeros on stack via 8 PUSH EAX\n2. Build character bitmap: Loop through lpszAccept, for each char c:\n   - Compute byte index: c >> 3 (divide by 8)\n   - Compute bit position: c & 7 (modulo 8)\n   - Set bit using BTS instruction: abCharBitmap[c >> 3] |= (1 << (c & 7))\n3. Scan lpszString: Loop through each character c:\n   - If c == 0 (null terminator), return NULL\n   - Test if c is in accept set using BT instruction\n   - If bit set (char found in accept set), return pointer to that character\n   - Otherwise advance to next character\n\nParameters:\n  lpszString (char *) - Null-terminated string to search\n  lpszAccept (char *) - Null-terminated set of characters to find\n\nReturns:\n  char * - Pointer to first character in lpszString that appears in lpszAccept\n  NULL   - If no character from lpszAccept is found in lpszString\n\nSpecial Cases:\n  - Empty accept set: Returns NULL (no characters to match)\n  - Empty string: Returns NULL immediately\n  - First char matches: Returns lpszString unchanged\n\nImplementation Notes:\n  Uses x86 BTS (Bit Test and Set) and BT (Bit Test) for efficient bitmap operations.\n  Stack bitmap avoids heap allocation. 256-bit map covers all possible byte values.\n  Optimized assembly: LEA ECX,[ECX] is NOP for alignment.",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.07",
